      "math_academy_id": "q-332579",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png)",
